## The idea

Each group must sum to **total / k**. Place the numbers **largest first** into groups, backtracking when stuck. Two prunings make it fast. Groups with the **same current sum** are interchangeable, so try only one of them. And if a number does not fit into an **empty** group, trying another empty group cannot help.

## Step by step

1. Reject if total % k ≠ 0 or any number > total / k.
2. Sort descending.
3. Place(i): try each group with room; skip a sum already tried at this level; recurse; undo.
4. After undoing from an empty group, stop trying more groups.
5. Follow-up: keep the numbers in lists alongside the sums and return them.

## Worked example

**Input:** nums = [4, 3, 2, 3, 5, 2, 1], k = 4 (target 5)

| Step | What happens |
|---|---|
| 5 | group A = 5 (full) |
| 4 | group B = 4 |
| 3, 3 | groups C = 3, D = 3 |
| 2, 2 | C = 5, D = 5 |
| 1 | B = 5 |

**Result:** true: [5], [4, 1], [3, 2], [3, 2]

## Cost

Exponential in the worst case (n ≤ 16); the prunings cut most of it.

## The code (C#, compiled and tested against every part of the question)

```csharp
// LinkedIn (reported Oct 2026) — LeetCode 698 Partition to K Equal Sum Subsets. n is small (≤ 16).
public static class KSubsets {
    public static bool CanPartition(int[] nums, int k) => Partition(nums, k) != null;

    // Follow-up: return the groups themselves (each list sums to total / k), or null when impossible.
    public static List<List<int>> Partition(int[] nums, int k) {
        if (nums == null || k <= 0 || nums.Length < k) return null;
        long total = 0; int max = 0;
        foreach (int x in nums) { total += x; if (x > max) max = x; }
        if (total % k != 0) return null;
        int target = (int)(total / k);
        if (max > target) return null;                      // one number alone is too big
        var sorted = (int[])nums.Clone();
        Array.Sort(sorted); Array.Reverse(sorted);          // big numbers first: dead ends are found sooner
        var sums = new int[k];
        var groups = new List<List<int>>();
        for (int g = 0; g < k; g++) groups.Add(new List<int>());
        return Place(sorted, 0, sums, groups, target) ? groups : null;
    }

    // Put sorted[i] into some group, then the rest. Backtracking with two prunings.
    private static bool Place(int[] nums, int i, int[] sums, List<List<int>> groups, int target) {
        if (i == nums.Length) return true;                  // every sum is target (total = k × target)
        var tried = new HashSet<int>();
        for (int g = 0; g < sums.Length; g++) {
            if (sums[g] + nums[i] > target) continue;
            if (!tried.Add(sums[g])) continue;              // two groups with the same sum are interchangeable
            sums[g] += nums[i]; groups[g].Add(nums[i]);
            if (Place(nums, i + 1, sums, groups, target)) return true;
            sums[g] -= nums[i]; groups[g].RemoveAt(groups[g].Count - 1);
            if (sums[g] == 0) break;                        // it did not fit an empty group: no other empty group helps
        }
        return false;
    }
}
```

## Likely follow-up questions

**Q: Quick rejects?**

total % k ≠ 0, or any number > total / k.

**Q: Why largest first?**

Big numbers have the fewest places to go, so dead ends show up early.

**Q: Complexity?**

O(k^n) worst case; with pruning far less. The bitmask DP is O(n · 2ⁿ).

**Q: Bitmask DP?**

dp[mask] = current group's partial sum for the numbers in mask; reachable if some unused number fits.

**Q: Shared state?**

None outside the call.

**Q: n = 40.**

Exponential either way; meet-in-the-middle helps for k = 2 only. Say so.

**Q: Related?**

Partition Equal Subset Sum (k = 2) is a knapsack DP — mention the special case.
